# Standardized Logging

## Single entry point

- All backend logs go through `createLogger` from `@app/shared` — **including API Gateway access logs**.
- Nest runtime logs use `NestStandardLogger` (wraps `createLogger`). Never `console.*` in handlers/use-cases/adapters.

```typescript
import { createLogger, logHttpRequest, NestStandardLogger } from '@app/shared';

const logger = createLogger('transactions');
logger.info('transaction.created', { transactionId });

// API access (same JSON envelope)
logHttpRequest({ service: 'api-gateway', message implied: 'http.request', ... });
```

## Standard shape

Every line is one JSON object:

`timestamp`, `level`, `service`, `message`, optional `correlationId` / `requestId`, optional `data` (redacted).

| Kind | `service` | `message` |
|---|---|---|
| Domain / Lambda | microservice name | event key e.g. `lambda.cold_start` |
| HTTP access (API) | `api-gateway` | `http.request` |

HTTP `data` includes: `channel: "http"`, `method`, `path`, `statusCode`, `durationMs`, `targetService`.

## Wiring

- Each Nest app: `AccessLogMiddleware(serviceName)` on all routes + `NestStandardLogger` in `NestFactory.create`.
- Propagate `x-correlation-id` on every response.
